KW  - Skeleton modeling
KW  - Hierarchical perception
KW  - Cross-scale embedding
KW  - Cross-modal
KW  - Person re-identification
AB  - A structurally stable skeleton prototype is key to fine-grained person re-identification. Existing methods fail to build feature associations across pedestrian skeleton sequences, which also lack cross-level representation capability and struggle to capture global limb information. As a result, the skeleton model cannot maintain the consistency of the skeleton characteristics across different modalities. To solve the above problems, this paper proposes a skeleton model based on hierarchical perception, which is composed of a hard skeleton extraction module (HSEM) and a cross-scale embedding module (CEM). The HSEM employs a skeleton hierarchical representation strategy to extract multi-level skeleton structures, and incorporates a local sub-sequence random masking mechanism to discover structurally stable skeleton prototypes under perturbations, thereby the discriminability of skeleton features can be enhanced by using prototype similarity. On this basis, hierarchical contrastive learning (HCL) is introduced to achieve hierarchical consistency reinforcement among similar features, and multi-level abstract features are mapped to subspaces at each level to enhance identity recognition capabilities. The CEM model leverages the self-attention mechanism to learn cross-scale positional deviation information across different skeleton levels, which can effectively align the local spatial representations between modalities and reduce modality discrepancies. Simultaneously, we propose a skeleton joint optimization loss function to enhance the consistency and discriminative power of cross-modal features. The experimental results demonstrate that the proposed approach significantly improves the performance on both the Sun Yat-sen university multiple modality re-identification version 1 (SYSU-MM01) and Dongguk body-based person recognition database (RegDB) datasets. In the SYSU-MM01 dataset, Rank-1 and mean average precision (mAP) reach 81.51 % and 77.17 %, respectively, whereas in the RegDB dataset, these metrics are 97.67 % and 97.33 %, respectively.
