KW  - Deep convolutional generative adversarial network
KW  - Pseudo-labeling method
AB  - Edge devices are increasingly preferred for gas pipeline leak detection because they avoid the high latency of traditional cloud architectures. However, deep-learning detectors often run slowly on resource-constrained hardware due to model complexity. Although lightweight designs reduce computation and speed inference, they can lower detection confidence. In addition, limited training diversity can weaken generalization across scenarios and conditions. We propose a lightweight model, MSE-DCGAN(MobileNetV3 Squeeze-and-Excitation Enhanced Intersection over Union-DCGAN), specifically designed for real-time detection of gas leaks on edge devices to address the aforementioned issues. Compared with existing deep learning based gas leak detection models, the biggest feature of MSE-DCGAN is to balance high confidence and low latency while improving the model's generalization ability. Specifically, MSE-DCGAN adopts MobileNetV3 as a lightweight backbone network to improve response speed, combines Squeeze and Excitation (SE) attention mechanism to enhance detection accuracy, uses Enhanced Intersection over Union (EIoU) loss function to ensure the stability of bounding box regression, and leverages the Deep Convolutional Generative Adversarial Network (DCGAN) to enrich the diversity of the dataset, combined with a semi-supervised pseudo-labeling training strategy to enhance the model's generalization ability on edge devices. The experimental results show that the average confidence of MSE-DCGAN in the test set is 0.9756, and the average response speed is 46.45 ms (ms). In addition, the model parameters proposed in this paper are only 8.11 megabytes (MB), which is very suitable for deployment on edge devices.
