KW  - Robust co-location pattern
KW  - Pruning strategy
KW  - Connection score
KW  - Road network
KW  - Multiple paths
AB  - Spatial co-location pattern mining (SCPM) is crucial for spatial association discovery, which aims to discover the subset of spatial features whose instances are frequently located in proximate areas, and it has broad prospects in many applications, such as ecology, public health, smart city, etc. SCPM in urban areas (SCPM-UA) discovers co-location patterns within urban areas by utilizing road network distance instead of Euclidean distance to assess the proximity relationships between instances. Existing SCPM-UA approaches assess the proximity relationships between instances via the shortest path solely, neglecting other proximity paths. Consequently, some robust co-location patterns may be missed. The robustness of a co-location pattern is determined by the number of paths through which its instances maintain proximity, directly reflecting the ability of patterns to resist emergencies. To find co-location patterns with high robustness in urban areas, we model proximity relationships between instances using paths with distance and overlap limitations. Furthermore, we design a Double-Bound Pruning (DBP) algorithm to identify these paths efficiently. Moreover, we introduce a novel metric called Connection Score, which considers multi-path proximity and traffic directions to quantify the robustness of co-location patterns, and then formally define the robust co-location patterns. Finally, we design a framework for robust co-location pattern mining based on DBP and Connection Score. Extensive experiments conducted on multiple datasets demonstrate that the Connection Score can identify robust co-location patterns missed by existing SCPM methods, and the efficiency of DBP is several times or even orders of magnitude better than competitors.
