AB  - Automatic segmentation of multiple brain metastases (MBMs) is extremely difficult because (1) the occurrence (1-191/patient), size (1-64 millimeters), and positions of multiple brain metastases vary significantly between/within patients and (2) multiple brain metastases exhibit low contrast with neighboring tissues, notably peritumoral edema (pixel difference of approximately 10.46). To mitigate the above issues, we propose a new bead nonlinear spiking neural (BNSN) P system with bead synapse and two types of new rules. Based on the new rules, three nonlinear spike regulation modules are designed to perform accurate segmentation on MBMs in parallelism. Specifically, a spike preprocessing strategy (SPS) is presented to suppress irrelevant background noise from multiple pulse inputs. Then, a neural communication interface (NCI) module is designed to impose constraints on the similarity of adjacent features to enhance fuzzy boundaries via a bead synapse information library, enabling the extraction of matching distributions during training. A global coordination rule (GCR) is also constructed to leverage global and local information across different levels of features in diverse multiple brain metastases. Experimental results on 1020 patients with 8166 multiple brain metastases yield a Dice Similarity Coefficient of 0.869. Experimental results demonstrate that bead nonlinear spiking neural P system is superior to seven state-of-the-art methods and shows potential in clinical applications.
