AB  - Cross-city resilience assessment is vital for multi-city collaborative governance. However, existing methods are limited to single-city and retrospective resilience assessments, lacking cross-city knowledge transfer and predictive modeling. To address these challenges, we propose a transfer learning-based framework for cross-city and predictive resilience assessment. First, we develop a cross-city spatio-temporal traffic prediction model, which transfers traffic patterns from source cities to target cities, enabling accurate traffic state forecasting in target cities. Second, we design a hysteresis resilience indicator, precisely characterizing the evolution of system performance during disturbances. Finally, we propose a fine-grained spatio-temporal resilience quantification algorithm. In the temporal dimension, we define performance retention rate, resistance index, and maximum recovery rate to enable phase-aware assessment. In the spatial dimension, quantification is refined to road-node, realizing high-resolution spatial assessment. Experimental results on San Jose and Beijing demonstrate that the proposed method accurately assesses resilience, providing forward-looking support for cross-city traffic management.
