AB  - This paper presents a novel automatic algorithm for preprocessing and enhancing raw digital mammography images. The proposed algorithm consists of three steps: removing additional margins in the background, removing labels, and extracting pectoral muscles (PM). These steps involve analyzing changes in rows, columns, and their means and a hybrid approach based on the Gaussian mixture model (GMM) and line equation. Furthermore, our algorithm includes two enhancement steps: darkening with an exponential function by determining the optimal value for its parameter, and contrast enhancement using a screened Poisson equation (SPE). The performance of the proposed algorithm is evaluated on the Mammography Image Analysis Society (MIAS), the Digital Database for Screening Mammography (DDSM), and INbreast datasets. Due to lack of ground truth data for the PM, additional margins, and labels to evaluate our algorithm, we use the data augmentation technique to simulate the ground truth of PM and define novel criteria for removing additional margins and labels. The Dice similarity coefficient and accuracy for the PM removal are 99.81 % and 99.92 % on the MIAS, 99.76 % and 99.88 % on the DDSM, and 99.69 % and 99.83 % on the INbreast. The results show that our algorithm effectively and accurately removes the unnecessary regions, extracts the region of interest (ROI), and standardizes the image quality without any adverse effects. In addition, the algorithm shows stability in the presence of noise in the preprocessing step. The comparative analysis shows that our proposed algorithm performs better than existing methods for image preprocessing and enhancement and provides superior results.
