AB  - The rapid growth of electric vehicles has underscored the importance of managing retired batteries effectively. Echelon utilization offers a promising pathway to extend battery life and enhance resource efficiency, with accurate screening serving as a crucial prerequisite. However, existing methods often overlook anomalous batteries—cells whose static parameters, such as internal resistance (IR) or incremental capacity (IC) peak coordinates, deviate significantly from the population and fail to integrate both static and dynamic performance. Static performance refers to scalar indicators, whereas dynamic performance captures temporal behaviors such as voltage–capacity (V–Q) and IC curves. This oversight leads to suboptimal classification outcomes. To address these challenges, this paper introduces a two-stage screening framework combining anomaly pre-screening, dynamic performance characterization, and image-based classification. First, a DBSCAN-based model identifies and excludes anomalous batteries. Next, dynamic features are converted into grayscale and Gramian Angular Field (GAF) images to capture nonlinear and temporal patterns comprehensively. Finally, an image classification framework enhanced with a convolutional block attention module (CBAM) highlights discriminative dynamic features, thereby improving classification accuracy. Experiments on the Massachusetts Institute of Technology battery dataset demonstrate that the proposed method achieves a screening accuracy of 91%, substantially outperforming conventional approaches.
