KW  - Land use land cover
AB  - Remote sensing image classification is critical in geospatial applications such as land cover mapping and environmental monitoring. While traditional Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) have demonstrated strong performance in this domain, but show reduced effectiveness when dealing with complex spectral and spatial variations in remote sensing data, creating a clear research gap in achieving both high accuracy and computational efficiency. To address this gap, the present study proposes a novel Artificial Intelligence (AI)-driven Quantum Convolutional Neural Network-based Hybrid Network (QCHNet), which seamlessly integrates quantum computing principles with classical deep learning architectures to significantly enhance classification performance. The engineering application of this model is demonstrated in the context of land use and land cover (LULC) classification using Linear Imaging Self-Scanning Sensor-III (LISS-III) multispectral satellite imagery, implemented on the Matrix Laboratory (MATLAB) Quantum Computing platform. Experimental results show that QCHNet achieved an overall classification accuracy of 96.54 %, outperforming both classical CNN (90.70 %) and Quantum CNN (94.27 %). Performance metrics such as precision (96.4 %), recall (97.1 %), and F1-score (96.8 %) further validated its effectiveness, despite modest training (1.26 h) and testing (0.62 h) overheads. This work demonstrates how AI-driven quantum-classical hybrid models can be effectively applied to solve engineering problems in remote sensing, pushing the boundaries of classification accuracy in complex geospatial datasets.
