AB  - High-precision gear fault diagnosis plays a vital role in industrial systems. However, the vibration signals collected from gears are often affected by strong noise interference. Currently, whether using mainstream single-sensor methods or multi-sensor diagnostic approaches based on cascade fusion, diagnostic accuracy is significantly compromised by noise interference. To address this issue, this paper proposes a Multi-sensor Cross-scale Time–Frequency Network for intelligent gear fault diagnosis. A wavelet reconstruction method that optimizes high-frequency coefficients by extracting regional variance is employed to fuse multi-sensor data. Empirical Mode Decomposition (EMD) and Wavelet Transform (WT) are used to generate time–frequency images from the fused signals. Cross-scale convolution and a dual-residual depthwise separable structure are applied to extract discriminative features, followed by classification. The proposed model was validated on two benchmark datasets. As a result, it achieved 99.70% diagnostic accuracy on the original signals, and maintained a high accuracy of 94.06% on signals corrupted with -6 dB Gaussian noise.
