KW  - Fair influence maximization
KW  - Overlapping community detection
KW  - Multi-transformation optimization
KW  - Evolutionary framework
AB  - Influence maximization is a critical problem in social network analysis that aims to identify a seed set of users to maximize the expected spread of influence. To avoid the high computational cost of Monte Carlo simulations, many existing studies employ low-cost transformation models to approximate the diffusion process. However, selecting a single appropriate transformation in advance is challenging due to the diverse characteristics of users and network structures. Moreover, current approaches often ignore the overlapping nature of communities, where influential nodes may participate in multiple groups. To address these issues, we propose MTO-FIM (Multi-Transformation Optimization for Fair Influence Maximization), a novel evolutionary framework designed to jointly optimize multiple influence transformations. Each transformation is treated as an independent task, assigned to a dedicated evolutionary solver. Furthermore, MTO-FIM leverages community structures to guide key evolutionary operations, ensuring that both overlapping and non-overlapping nodes are fairly considered in the process of identifying the most influential seed nodes. In general, the proposed algorithm consists of three key components: (1) estimating the potential relationship between transformations based on user overlap across populations, (2) enabling information exchange among transformations proportional to their relationship strength, and (3) selecting a final seed set by integrating insights from all transformations while ensuring fair consideration of overlapping and non-overlapping nodes. Experimental results on real-world social networks show that MTO-FIM outperforms state-of-the-art baselines in both influence spread and fairness. Specifically, it achieves an average improvement of 8.1 % in influence coverage by effectively leveraging transferable knowledge across transformation models.
