AB  - Existing supervised action segmentation methods rely heavily on the quality of frame-wise classification using attention mechanisms or temporal convolutions. Even boundary correction approaches depend on the accuracy of initial frame-wise classification, which may fail to precisely identify action boundaries under low-quality predictions. To address this limitation, we propose ASESM (Action Segmentation via Explicit Similarity Measurement), a framework that improves action segmentation accuracy by explicitly evaluating similarity across frames and predictions. Frame-level multi-resolution temporal features are processed by multiple Transformer encoders to generate diverse frame-wise predictions, which are then refined via similarity-based voting to obtain a high-quality initial prediction. We also introduce a boundary correction algorithm that iteratively refines boundaries based on feature similarity between consecutive frames during training. As a post-processing step, a smoothing technique eliminates outlier classes within segments based on consistency between consecutive predictions. We further extend the boundary correction mechanism to an unsupervised setting by proposing a training-free boundary detection and correction algorithm that identifies segment boundaries solely based on feature similarity. Experiments on the 50Salads, Georgia Tech Egocentric Activities (GTEA), and Breakfast datasets demonstrate the effectiveness of the proposed method in both supervised and unsupervised scenarios. Code and models are available.11https://github.com/adjkamel/segsim.
