KW  - Fuzzy set
KW  - Intuitionistic fuzzy sets
KW  - Pythagorean fuzzy sets
KW  - q-Rung orthopair fuzzy sets
KW  - Crime linkage
KW  - Mental disorder
AB  - Crime remains an unavoidable reality, posing a significant threat to societal norms and individual safety, and the growing backlog of unresolved cases highlights the need for efficient criminal investigations. Advanced methodologies and models, such as data mining and fuzzy set theory, are essential in addressing these challenges. Many of these crimes are committed by the same offenders, so the process of crime linkage becomes important in the criminal investigation process. Also, understanding the role of mental disorders — such as psychotic disorders, dementia, mood disorders, anxiety disorders, and impulse control disorders — in criminal behavior is equally important, as these disorders can significantly affect decision making and emotional regulation, potentially leading to unlawful actions. This study introduces the (m,n)-Generalized power root fuzzy set as an extension of existing fuzzy set theories, such as Intuitionistic fuzzy sets, Pythagorean fuzzy sets, and q-Rung orthopair fuzzy sets, to enhance decision-making under uncertainty. We propose a novel separation measure based on (m,n)-Generalized power root fuzzy set. The proposed separation measure is a generalized form of distance measure that can calculate the common dissimilarity for more than two values. In the study, we have provided a detailed analysis of its advantages over existing measures. The study outlines a new methodology for crime linkage using the proposed separation measure, supported by a case study with comparative and sensitive analysis demonstrating its superiority, applicability and effectiveness. In addition, we develop an innovative approach for the diagnosis of mental disorders in criminals using the proposed distance measure. The validity, superiority, and applicability of this approach are demonstrated through two case studies of mental disorders, supported by comparative, sensitivity, and statistical analysis. The paper concludes with a discussion of the findings and implications, underscoring the potential for data-driven approaches to transform criminology and criminal justice outcomes.
