KW  - Video instance segmentation
KW  - Collaborative supervision
KW  - Spatio-temporal manipulation
AB  - Due to the high labor cost introduced by intensive pixel-level annotation under the fully supervised framework, the balance between annotation complexity and segmentation performance has become an urgent demand for video instance segmentation. This paper proposes a cost-effective method called Collaboratively Supervised Video Instance Segmentation (CS-VIS). Firstly, we propose a novel collaborative supervision framework to obtain training supervision information from sparse mask-annotated and dense box-annotated instance samples, alleviating acute dependence on an extra pre-training network and corresponding atypically annotated samples of current non-fully supervised methods. Secondly, the temporal dynamic information in video frames is applied to manipulate the supervision weights among pixel-level samples in category training, optimizing the category response to dynamic instances. Finally, we put forward the theory of consistency propagation, according to which the spatial semantic fluctuation information is introduced in the spatial manipulation loss that effectively reduces supervision noise under box-level annotation. Experiments are conducted on the YouTube-VIS (YT-VIS) dataset to verify the video instance segmentation performance of CS-VIS, where mask annotation and bounding-box annotation are carried out on instances in the first frames and remaining frames of the YT-VIS training set respectively for collaborative supervision. We demonstrate that by combining the spatio-temporal manipulation and collaborative supervision mechanism, CS-VIS is trained from sparse mask-annotated and dense box-annotated samples in a cost-effective paradigm and the performance is competitive to part of the fully supervised methods, which provides advanced guidance for video instance segmentation and is of great academic significance in the development of non-fully supervised tasks.
