AB  - Accurate classification of cattle body conditions is critical for informed decision-making in livestock management and commercial transactions. However, traditional visual assessments are subjective and inconsistent. This study introduces a novel intelligent framework that integrates anatomical keypoint detection with Type-3 Fuzzy Sets (T3FS) for cattle body condition classification into three categories: thin, medium, and fat. The key novelty of this work lies in coupling the You Only Look Once version 8 (YOLOv8) pose model with a higher-order fuzzy inference system, bridging the gap between data-driven precision and the ability to handle labeling uncertainty. The pipeline begins with keypoint detection using a YOLOv8 pose model trained on field images, achieving a precision of 0.998 and a mean Average Precision (mAP) at multiple Intersection Over Union (IoU) thresholds from 0.5 to 0.95 (mAP@0.5:0.95) of 0.942 for bounding boxes, and 0.824 for keypoints, confirming its robustness under variable conditions. Morphometric features were extracted via Euclidean distances between keypoints and used to train two parallel classifiers: a Multilayer Perceptron (MLP) network and a T3FS-based model. The MLP network outperformed the conventional models, reaching 72.87 % accuracy and an F1-score of 0.711. Meanwhile, the T3FS model, enhanced with the Synthetic Minority Oversampling Technique and Edited Nearest Neighbors (SMOTE-ENN) and symbolic rule generation, achieved superior results with 78.49 % accuracy and a weighted F1-score of 0.81. The proposed framework provides a robust, interpretable, and non-invasive artificial intelligence system for cattle body condition classification. The system enables a real-time image analysis, ensuring scalability and practical applicability in precision livestock farming.
