KW  - Bi-matrix game
KW  - Probabilistic linguistic term set
KW  - Group decision making
KW  - Evidential reasoning
KW  - Quadratic programming model
AB  - The classical bi-matrix game has two basic characteristics that the payoffs are deterministic and the players are homogeneous individuals, which might result in its limited application to complex decision problems in artificial intelligence. To solve this issue, this paper investigates a probabilistic linguistic group bi-matrix game (PLGBMG) by introducing two groups of experts (actors or decision-makers). The two “players” are represented by the two groups of experts, and the payoffs given by each expert are sketched by probabilistic linguistic term sets (PLTSs). A new hesitancy index and a cosine similarity measure are defined for PLTSs. Then, a bi-objective optimization model is erected to determine the weights of experts in each group. An evidential reasoning-based approach is developed to aggregate the probabilistic linguistic payoff matrices of the individual experts of each group into a collective payoff matrix. Subsequently, to derive the solution of PLGBMG, this article builds a probabilistic linguistic quadratic programming model which is further converted into a triangular fuzzy quadratic programming model by using triangular fuzzy envelope of PLTSs. Ultimately, the developed PLGBMG is validated by two case studies on green mining construction and new energy vehicle industry development. Comparative analyses reveal the flexibility and superiority of the PLGBMG.
