AB  - Accurate commodity recognition is of paramount importance for the primary perceive information input of robotic sorting. However, ensuring 24/7 uninterrupted operation in low-light illumination while maintaining energy efficiency remains a critical challenge. However, existing methods often suffer from low recognition accuracy and poor adaptability under low-light illumination. Therefore, this paper, based on the You Only Look Once version 8 (YOLOv8) framework, proposes one day-night cross-modal commodity recognition network (DNC-YOLO). The proposed network consists of three key components: 1) A parallel skeleton architecture designed to separately extract red-green-blue (RGB) and infrared (IR) features, with shallow fusion enabling complementary representations for robust low-light adaptation; 2) A Hierarchical Semantic Fusion (HSF) module that enhances deep cross-level feature interaction to improve detection of multi-scale commodities; and 3) A Context-Aware Task Head (CATH) integrated with a distance-weighted Intersection over Union (IoU) to enhance discrimination among similarly shaped commodities and refine localization accuracy. Experimental results on the self-built commodity-grasping dataset demonstrate that DNC-YOLO achieves better performance, with a mean Average Precision (mAP) of 0.817 at IoU 0.50:0.95 and 0.979 at IoU 0.50, surpassing both single-modal and multi-modal state-of-the-art (SOTA) methods. This study not only provides a reliable solution for robotic sorting systems under challenging illumination but also highlights the novelty of integrating cross-modal learning, hierarchical semantic fusion, and context-aware localization in a unified framework, offering significant potential for intelligent retail applications. The code can be available in https://github.com/987189263/DNC-YOLO.
