AB  - Existing landslide early warning models, including physical mechanism models, rainfall statistics models and deformation characteristics models, typically compare monitoring values with preset thresholds to determine the warning level. However, when confronted with massive landslide sites exhibiting diverse geological formations, triggering factors and destabilization critical values, these models often encounter difficulties in setting appropriate thresholds. This paper presents a novel landslide early warning model for local system based on multi-source monitoring data using unsupervised machine learning. Various monitoring parameters were subjected to data preprocessing, wavelet decomposition, unsupervised machine learning, and comprehensive evaluation. Three algorithms were compared: Isolation Forest, One-Class Support Vector Machine and Local Outlier Factor. The Liannan landslide was selected as a case study to conduct the entire process, which indicated that the Isolation Forest algorithm outperformed the others in terms of integrated warning; therefore, this algorithm was selected for multi-source data processing. The model was then applied to 14 landslide sites to check its performance. It was proven to be capable of providing quantifiable warning levels at the time of significant deformation, which correlated well with the actual deformation trend and enabled the delivery of accurate warning levels. The proposed model can be applied to a wider range of landslide monitoring sites, and the data processing flow, thresholds and key parameters have a high degree of consistency, helping to address the challenge of setting thresholds for first-failure landslides. Besides, the model enables multiple parameters to be organically combined, and the occurrence of false alarms is visibly reduced.
