AB  - Accurate segmentation of skin lesions is essential for computer-aided diagnosis and treatment planning, yet it remains challenging due to irregular shapes, scale variations, and blurred boundaries. These factors make it difficult for models to preserve fine-grained local details while also capturing long-range semantic context. To address this issue, we propose an edge-aware segmentation network that integrates convolutional feature perception with visual state space modeling. The network employs a dual-branch encoder to balance the representation of local textures and edges with global semantic dependencies, while lightweight boundary refinement modules enhance contour delineation in the decoding stage. This design mitigates the weakness of state space modeling in capturing local details and enhances boundary localization. We evaluate the proposed method on four publicly available datasets and compare it against advanced segmentation approaches. Experimental results demonstrate that our network consistently achieves higher Dice similarity coefficient and intersection over union, as well as a lower 95 % Hausdorff distance. Furthermore, statistical tests confirm that these improvements are significant, providing strong evidence of the effectiveness of our approach for skin lesion segmentation. The source code is available at https://github.com/Randy158/SCE_Net.
