AB  - Clothing-change person re-identification (CC-ReID) aims to match the same pedestrian with different clothes from the non-overlapping surveillance cameras. CC-ReID in artificial intelligence engineering applications tends to suffer from a lack of real-world clothing diversity, which makes it difficult to learn the discriminative pedestrian features from cloth-changing scenarios. To address this problem, we propose a clothing semantic-driven progressive guidance learning (CGL) method to enhance clothing diversity in CC-ReID tasks. On the one hand, we explore the class proxy to represent the overall clothes semantics of all the images with the same pedestrian, and the masked clothing features gradually approach the class proxy in the feature space through progressive guidance learning in each training iteration. This approach dynamically and continuously captures more hierarchical clothes feature representations based on the provided limited real-world clothing. On the other hand, we fuse the original images of each pedestrian with the corresponding class proxy and perform multiple positive feature learning on the fused features to further enhance the capability of clothing feature representation from the feature space perspective. Extensive experiments on four commonly used person re-identification datasets show that our method outperforms the state-of-the-art approaches.
